1
00:00:01,972 --> 00:00:04,193
[SPEAKER_00]: It's hard to make a practice out of reflecting correctly.

2
00:00:05,413 --> 00:00:10,274
[SPEAKER_00]: It's eh... it does... a lot of it come down to repetition and drilling.

3
00:00:11,214 --> 00:00:14,595
[SPEAKER_00]: So, going through the... through the... not necessarily just through the motions.

4
00:00:14,615 --> 00:00:19,996
[SPEAKER_00]: You might have to start with going through the motions, but really... which can also be done even... even for reflection.

5
00:00:20,016 --> 00:00:21,777
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what happens to people sometimes.

6
00:00:25,098 --> 00:00:29,779
[SPEAKER_00]: Initially, you establish your mind upon certain reflexive attitude, upon certain context or a topic.

7
00:00:30,564 --> 00:00:41,188
[SPEAKER_00]: But then, afterwards, you just want to almost like have a shortcut to it and then just go through the mental motions and expect the same establishment.

8
00:00:42,088 --> 00:00:46,009
[SPEAKER_00]: But, needless to say, it doesn't work or it stops working.

9
00:00:46,029 --> 00:00:48,190
[SPEAKER_00]: That's when you know that your practice became mechanical.

10
00:00:48,890 --> 00:01:00,334
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's when you either have to shift up the approach or, again, re-include your attempts at the shortcut into the present situation.

11
00:01:01,800 --> 00:01:24,089
[SPEAKER_00]: So, in other words, if, say, for example, the context of your establishment of the mind is a mind above sensuality, and you want to be always starting, any reflection for that matter, doesn't matter the context, doesn't matter the topic you're trying to contemplate, you want to be doing it on the basis of the presently enduring situation.

12
00:01:24,469 --> 00:01:25,590
[SPEAKER_00]: So, right here, right now.

13
00:01:28,392 --> 00:01:33,313
[SPEAKER_00]: But you don't just say, oh, I'm right here, right now, as a matter of fact.

14
00:01:33,333 --> 00:01:43,556
[SPEAKER_00]: You... you have to... that kind of recognition needs to be rooted in how you feel right here, right now.

15
00:01:44,977 --> 00:01:48,238
[SPEAKER_00]: Because, as the Buddha said, things converge on the level of feeling.

16
00:01:49,038 --> 00:01:55,860
[SPEAKER_00]: So... and that's exactly when your practice or meditation becomes mechanical.

17
00:01:57,179 --> 00:02:00,380
[SPEAKER_00]: It's, eh... when it's not rooted in how you're feeling presently.

18
00:02:00,601 --> 00:02:04,202
[SPEAKER_00]: How... when it's not rooted in the presently enduring feeling.

19
00:02:06,043 --> 00:02:07,444
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the accurate way of putting it.

20
00:02:08,564 --> 00:02:26,452
[SPEAKER_00]: So, how you're feeling right now, and, eh... if the context, as I said, if the context is the practice of, eh... establishing the mind above the domain of centrality, eh... based on the currently enduring feeling, so, say, it's a neutral feeling, or say, it's a pleasant feeling, as in pleasant as a good mood,

21
00:02:27,440 --> 00:02:34,608
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you want to, eh... again, as the Buddha often encouraged, you want to review your mind, not necessarily psychologically analyze it.

22
00:02:34,768 --> 00:02:42,036
[SPEAKER_00]: Just stay on the level of the presently enduring feeling, that's enduring there on its own, eh... for this whole time now.

23
00:02:43,017 --> 00:02:46,481
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, you want to review your mind in a sense of, eh...

24
00:02:47,542 --> 00:03:02,655
[SPEAKER_00]: See, foresee and reconsider whether you would be liable, whether that mind would be liable to sensual desire or to ill-will, because those things are on the same domain.

25
00:03:02,675 --> 00:03:05,818
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you can simply ask yourself that question.

26
00:03:05,878 --> 00:03:12,864
[SPEAKER_00]: So, right here, right now, while I'm sitting in this room, having a pleasant mood enduring,

27
00:03:14,272 --> 00:03:19,315
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's the... that's the establishment of the situation based on the... what present feeling endures.

28
00:03:20,736 --> 00:03:24,638
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't need to rush for the answer, as I often say.

29
00:03:24,658 --> 00:03:26,139
[SPEAKER_00]: So, allow this situation to endure.

30
00:03:26,579 --> 00:03:30,522
[SPEAKER_00]: Don't mystify it, but certainly don't try to provide answers for it.

31
00:03:32,683 --> 00:03:43,029
[SPEAKER_00]: If you have any doubt, so now there's doubt, you can ask, well, am I still feeling a positive or a good... good to neutral mood, whichever way you want to describe it.

32
00:03:44,360 --> 00:03:47,041
[SPEAKER_00]: And if the answer is yes, means the same mood is still enduring.

33
00:03:48,802 --> 00:03:54,265
[SPEAKER_00]: So that's... that's the important... important bit to... to discern and then allow it to endure.

34
00:03:54,646 --> 00:03:59,208
[SPEAKER_00]: So then, even your doubt is secondary to how you're feeling right here, right now.

35
00:04:00,169 --> 00:04:06,252
[SPEAKER_00]: Again, if you're not rooted at the point where things converge, which is at the point of present enduring feeling, that's when doubt can take over you.

36
00:04:06,992 --> 00:04:09,794
[SPEAKER_00]: But, as I said, you just have to re-include it.

37
00:04:10,454 --> 00:04:12,255
[SPEAKER_00]: Include doubt in the present enduring feeling.

38
00:04:14,609 --> 00:04:25,272
[SPEAKER_00]: And, eh... even if it's unpleasant then, you surmount it by... by being mindful of the... of the feeling that's there beforehand, pretty much, because you're doubting your mood only because that mood is there.

39
00:04:25,752 --> 00:04:29,093
[SPEAKER_00]: Or you're doubting how you might be feeling, because you must be feeling something.

40
00:04:30,554 --> 00:04:31,134
[SPEAKER_00]: So, eh...

41
00:04:32,394 --> 00:04:44,158
[SPEAKER_00]: So, in the specific context of establishing mind above sensuality, even temporarily, so to speak, you have to aim at establishing your mind, eh... above the possibility of sensuality.

42
00:04:45,498 --> 00:04:56,504
[SPEAKER_00]: And that already means that you have been living, certainly for a sufficient amount of time, certainly invested a sufficient amount of effort, living withdrawn from sensuality bodily.

43
00:04:57,845 --> 00:05:08,691
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if your acts are not withdrawn from sensuality bodily, as in, if there is no virtue, basis of virtue and basis of sensitive strain, then you're not going to be discerning anything of the... any of this on the level of the mind.

44
00:05:09,371 --> 00:05:10,272
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's why the...

45
00:05:12,042 --> 00:05:14,664
[SPEAKER_00]: The virtue needs to come first.

46
00:05:15,304 --> 00:05:18,987
[SPEAKER_00]: Your sīla, the precept, is not the dhamma, but there is no dhamma without it.

47
00:05:20,128 --> 00:05:21,188
[SPEAKER_00]: And this is exactly why.

48
00:05:22,589 --> 00:05:31,836
[SPEAKER_00]: You cannot possibly discern the liability to sensuality on the mental level, when you're fully immersed in it physically.

49
00:05:31,956 --> 00:05:36,539
[SPEAKER_00]: Or certainly, when you haven't been withdrawn from it long enough physically.

50
00:05:38,796 --> 00:05:54,184
[SPEAKER_00]: So, taking for granted that you have been withdrawn long enough physically, as in you have been keeping the precepts, then you are creating a certain, well, to an extent, some degree, a degree of a basis for this discernment.

51
00:05:54,204 --> 00:05:55,705
[SPEAKER_00]: And discernment is as follows.

52
00:05:55,725 --> 00:05:58,587
[SPEAKER_00]: You just ask yourself, on the basis of present enduring feeling,

53
00:05:59,667 --> 00:06:02,069
[SPEAKER_00]: Am I liable to sense desire?

54
00:06:02,370 --> 00:06:20,448
[SPEAKER_00]: As in, is this mind, that's presently enduring this currently positive, pleasant feeling, is it liable to be sort of taken up by essential craving, gripped by it, under the pressure of essential desire?

55
00:06:22,369 --> 00:06:23,571
[SPEAKER_00]: If you're not sure,

56
00:06:24,503 --> 00:06:25,363
[SPEAKER_00]: Means it is.

57
00:06:25,844 --> 00:06:30,425
[SPEAKER_00]: So, not being clear that you are free from it, to that extent you are not free from it.

58
00:06:32,286 --> 00:06:35,608
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you can review the possibility, and that's already what sensuality is, by the way.

59
00:06:35,768 --> 00:06:38,869
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't need to be reviewing possibility and then waiting for sensuality to come.

60
00:06:38,889 --> 00:06:39,969
[SPEAKER_00]: There is no sensuality to come.

61
00:06:39,989 --> 00:06:45,492
[SPEAKER_00]: Sensuality stays on the level of possibility, or rather begins already on the level of possibility.

62
00:06:45,512 --> 00:06:50,454
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you deal with it over there, then you wouldn't have to be dealing only on the level of physical and senses.

63
00:06:51,328 --> 00:06:55,689
[SPEAKER_00]: Because possibility to sensual desire is already sensuality.

64
00:06:56,069 --> 00:06:57,289
[SPEAKER_00]: It's already sensual desire.

65
00:06:57,809 --> 00:07:00,050
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's what we mentioned in other talks.

66
00:07:00,350 --> 00:07:06,371
[SPEAKER_00]: You can imagine a possibility of sensual engagement and that's already where your gratification begins.

67
00:07:06,471 --> 00:07:14,393
[SPEAKER_00]: Begins on the level of the thought, of the prospect of sensual engagement, of the prospect that is actually unpleasant.

68
00:07:16,371 --> 00:07:32,498
[SPEAKER_00]: Because if the prospect of sensual engagement were already pleasant, felt pleasantly, eh... at the at the fundamental primal level, eh... the desire towards what that prospect directs you would be inconceivable, because you're already within the satisfied pleasure.

69
00:07:33,219 --> 00:07:34,079
[SPEAKER_00]: But that's the whole point.

70
00:07:34,179 --> 00:07:44,164
[SPEAKER_00]: It's felt unpleasantly, and it directs you in a direction towards sense object, that will make this unpleasantness less, be felt less.

71
00:07:45,533 --> 00:07:53,742
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's why people end up developing a gratuitous assumption that that which you get down that direction is pleasant.

72
00:07:53,982 --> 00:07:54,463
[SPEAKER_00]: But it isn't.

73
00:07:54,683 --> 00:07:58,828
[SPEAKER_00]: It's just slightly less unpleasant, but it's equally within the same unpleasantness.

74
00:08:00,579 --> 00:08:07,361
[SPEAKER_00]: And the important point here, to start seeing this direction, is to recognize that first you start with sense of pleasure.

75
00:08:07,741 --> 00:08:20,305
[SPEAKER_00]: So, for a mind that's not free from sensuality, you cannot possibly see it as unpleasant first, and then you can sort of emulate seeing of that, and that's a good way to... that's an inevitable way to start.

76
00:08:21,086 --> 00:08:21,826
[SPEAKER_00]: But the point is,

77
00:08:22,980 --> 00:08:28,521
[SPEAKER_00]: Factually speaking, on the basis of your present enduring experience as a whole, sensuality is felt pleasantly.

78
00:08:28,601 --> 00:08:29,582
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the first step.

79
00:08:30,362 --> 00:08:33,403
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's why it's important to practice sense restraint.

80
00:08:33,543 --> 00:08:42,505
[SPEAKER_00]: Because if you're not allowed, if you're not able to allow the... that pleasure to manifest for what it is,

81
00:08:43,703 --> 00:08:44,664
[SPEAKER_00]: You won't be able to see it.

82
00:08:44,984 --> 00:08:53,148
[SPEAKER_00]: And by not being able to allow pleasure to manifest for what it is, means not having practiced sensory strain sufficiently enough, or well enough.

83
00:08:53,748 --> 00:08:59,171
[SPEAKER_00]: So, as soon as this pleasure starts... the prospect of this pleasure starts to increase, you're bound to give in.

84
00:08:59,831 --> 00:09:07,075
[SPEAKER_00]: So, again, that's why sīla needs to be developed first, so that you can then allow this thing to arise, and then understand it for what it is.

85
00:09:07,775 --> 00:09:09,996
[SPEAKER_00]: See the pain in it, inherent.

86
00:09:10,177 --> 00:09:12,638
[SPEAKER_00]: The heart of it is always painful, cannot be different.

87
00:09:13,611 --> 00:09:24,917
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you would be able to allow that, if you haven't been habitually, automatically, carelessly acting on the level of the physical, based on that desire.

88
00:09:25,397 --> 00:09:31,161
[SPEAKER_00]: So, basically, if you haven't been given in to the sensuality on the level of your body.

89
00:09:34,322 --> 00:09:39,065
[SPEAKER_00]: If you're able to allow that pleasure that's felt first,

90
00:09:40,408 --> 00:09:51,681
[SPEAKER_00]: It's then within the basis of that enduring prospect of pleasure, which is pleasant, within that basis you get to understand the pressure that it fundamentally exerts on you.

91
00:09:51,761 --> 00:09:55,906
[SPEAKER_00]: And you can ask yourself, you being pressured, is that pleasant or unpleasant?

92
00:09:56,086 --> 00:09:57,167
[SPEAKER_00]: It's always unpleasant.

93
00:09:58,789 --> 00:09:59,530
[SPEAKER_00]: Always unpleasant.

94
00:09:59,650 --> 00:09:59,870
[SPEAKER_00]: So...

95
00:10:00,773 --> 00:10:09,562
[SPEAKER_00]: Within the presently enduring pleasure of the prospect of sensuality, you get to understand that it's always fundamentally felt painfully.

96
00:10:10,282 --> 00:10:11,383
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's the important thing.

97
00:10:11,964 --> 00:10:15,567
[SPEAKER_00]: For as long as you assume, even to the tiniest of the degrees, that

98
00:10:16,268 --> 00:10:28,514
[SPEAKER_00]: Pleasure of the prospect of sensuality is in the direction of these sense objects, even tiny little bit more than it's on the level of the presently enduring painful pressure that it exerts over you.

99
00:10:28,614 --> 00:10:31,416
[SPEAKER_00]: To that extent, you assume it to be different.

100
00:10:32,176 --> 00:10:41,021
[SPEAKER_00]: To that extent, you give it its own being, being of sensuality, as in being that pertains to over that that thing over there, that is being promised as the object of your sense.

101
00:10:42,634 --> 00:10:51,059
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's why, once you're able to allow that pleasure to rise, so you mustn't be like, I must get rid of this, I must get rid of this, and you certainly mustn't be giving in physically.

102
00:10:51,219 --> 00:11:01,725
[SPEAKER_00]: Once you're able to allow it to endure, you will see that the pleasure that you feel of that endurance, is actually the pain of its pressure.

103
00:11:02,286 --> 00:11:05,948
[SPEAKER_00]: It's the same thing, literally, just the other coin of the same thing.

104
00:11:06,775 --> 00:11:16,220
[SPEAKER_00]: And at that point, it will be impossible for you to regard that prospect of pleasure as anything other than painful.

105
00:11:17,360 --> 00:11:23,103
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's exactly what the Buddha described in the in the suttas we mentioned in previous talks as well, in Majjhima Nikaya.

106
00:11:23,795 --> 00:11:28,817
[SPEAKER_00]: The point being, eh... engagement with sense object was always actually unpleasant.

107
00:11:29,417 --> 00:11:29,717
[SPEAKER_00]: Always.

108
00:11:30,377 --> 00:11:47,443
[SPEAKER_00]: So... and you will know that it was always unpleasant because, again, ask yourself, can you even conceive going down the direction of a sense object, pursuing it, eh... engaging with it, eh... using it for gratification, if there is no desire for it?

109
00:11:48,993 --> 00:11:54,018
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the context of your engagement with sense object is always the desire of the sense.

110
00:11:54,799 --> 00:11:55,099
[SPEAKER_00]: Always.

111
00:11:55,179 --> 00:11:55,960
[SPEAKER_00]: It's inconceivable.

112
00:11:56,240 --> 00:12:00,665
[SPEAKER_00]: Because if you engage with a sense object without a desire for the sense object, that's not centrality.

113
00:12:00,725 --> 00:12:02,266
[SPEAKER_00]: That's not an engagement.

114
00:12:02,286 --> 00:12:05,890
[SPEAKER_00]: There is no lust and aversion... basis for lust and aversion in it.

115
00:12:07,367 --> 00:12:18,335
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's why you recognize that the context, peripheral context of your presently directed mind towards sense object is always unpleasant pressure of that sense desire.

116
00:12:19,275 --> 00:12:35,447
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if that is the inevitable, inseparable, unremovable, irremovable context of any sensual direction, means any sensual direction is then that which pressures you because of which it's painful.

117
00:12:36,527 --> 00:12:54,815
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's important to not create a distinction, as I said, temporal distinction or even spatial distinction for that matter, between the pleasure that you feel first, on the prospect of sensuality, and the underlying inherent pressure it exerts over you, which is the pain.

118
00:12:55,955 --> 00:13:01,858
[SPEAKER_00]: So, then the prospect of pleasure is directly determined by the prospect of pressure.

119
00:13:03,851 --> 00:13:08,434
[SPEAKER_00]: In other words, pleasure you feel, it's actually the pain that pressures you.

120
00:13:09,174 --> 00:13:15,839
[SPEAKER_00]: And the reason you feel it pleasant, is because the direction offered, is the direction of possible escape from it.

121
00:13:16,399 --> 00:13:27,847
[SPEAKER_00]: So, again, imagine that, like you're in prison, you're being tortured, and suddenly, a thought occurs in your mind, that allows a possibility of escape from the torture.

122
00:13:28,724 --> 00:13:37,226
[SPEAKER_00]: Now, if the possibility of escape from the presently enduring exerted torture over you, if that possibility is slim, well, you're not going to get very elated.

123
00:13:37,786 --> 00:13:40,167
[SPEAKER_00]: It's going to be... but yeah...

124
00:13:40,547 --> 00:13:44,888
[SPEAKER_00]: But imagine that possibility being really quite realistic.

125
00:13:46,008 --> 00:13:49,149
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, you will have trouble containing your excitement.

126
00:13:50,049 --> 00:13:53,530
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's exactly the definition of sensuality when it comes down to senses.

127
00:13:55,411 --> 00:13:56,811
[SPEAKER_00]: Imprisoned by the desire,

128
00:13:57,830 --> 00:14:03,500
[SPEAKER_00]: That then determines the extent of pleasure you will feel, as in the extent of possible escape from it.

129
00:14:03,600 --> 00:14:07,186
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the greater the desire, the greater possibility of escape.

130
00:14:08,227 --> 00:14:11,029
[SPEAKER_00]: And thus, for the ignorant mind, the greater pleasure.

131
00:14:11,609 --> 00:14:12,270
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's what I mean.

132
00:14:12,390 --> 00:14:15,191
[SPEAKER_00]: You have to admit that there is this great pleasure or medium pleasure.

133
00:14:15,231 --> 00:14:16,112
[SPEAKER_00]: Pleasure is there.

134
00:14:16,712 --> 00:14:22,956
[SPEAKER_00]: Once you admit, you will get to see that pleasure is felt pleasantly, because it's determined by the pain.

135
00:14:23,596 --> 00:14:28,139
[SPEAKER_00]: In other words, the pain is felt pleasantly.

136
00:14:28,199 --> 00:14:34,323
[SPEAKER_00]: The underlying pain, the peripheral pain, the context of pain is felt pleasantly, because you're looking away from it.

137
00:14:35,752 --> 00:14:38,805
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's, again, that's exactly in the sutta that we quoted before.

138
00:14:39,596 --> 00:14:49,280
[SPEAKER_00]: The Buddha said, it was that... gave the simile of the leper and using the burning embers to cauterize his scratching wound, that offers him a sense of relief.

139
00:14:49,340 --> 00:14:53,763
[SPEAKER_00]: And he said, the Buddha said, the fire and burning embers were always painful.

140
00:14:54,403 --> 00:14:54,703
[SPEAKER_00]: Always.

141
00:14:55,423 --> 00:15:00,726
[SPEAKER_00]: The only reason that he was finding it pleasurable, genuinely, otherwise he wouldn't be doing it in the first place.

142
00:15:01,226 --> 00:15:07,349
[SPEAKER_00]: A leper would not have been going to the embers if they were not providing him at least some degree of pleasure.

143
00:15:07,969 --> 00:15:10,870
[SPEAKER_00]: But I would have said that pleasure was always actually painful.

144
00:15:11,310 --> 00:15:20,852
[SPEAKER_00]: The only reason he was finding a relief in it, delighting in it, being happy on account of it, was because of his condition of leprosy.

145
00:15:21,612 --> 00:15:21,952
[SPEAKER_00]: That's it.

146
00:15:22,032 --> 00:15:29,254
[SPEAKER_00]: And condition of leprosy is the condition of ignorance in regard to your own sensuality, in regard to your own pressure of the sense desire.

147
00:15:29,774 --> 00:15:36,596
[SPEAKER_00]: Because once the leper learns that the first thing he must do in order to cure his leprosy is stop scratching it and stop burning it with fire.

148
00:15:38,487 --> 00:15:41,209
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, he'll get to realize that, yeah, that truly is the way out.

149
00:15:41,429 --> 00:15:51,475
[SPEAKER_00]: So, sensory strain must be first, and then you get to understand that what I used to do, what you used to do, on account of escaping from sensuality, was actually just making sensuality worse.

150
00:15:52,255 --> 00:15:56,978
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you were just, eh... by being pressured from pain, that you were feeling pleasantly,

151
00:15:59,030 --> 00:16:05,871
[SPEAKER_00]: You were causing yourself even more pain, that was then felt even more pleasantly, that was making you cause even more pain, that was being felt even more pleasantly.

152
00:16:06,312 --> 00:16:07,852
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why sensuality is endless.

153
00:16:08,112 --> 00:16:13,973
[SPEAKER_00]: You can't satisfy your desires, and you certainly will never run out of preferences.

154
00:16:14,893 --> 00:16:18,894
[SPEAKER_00]: The mind will always keep pushing further and further and further, if that's the direction it goes.

155
00:16:19,234 --> 00:16:22,195
[SPEAKER_00]: And it can go infinitely, because that's not the direction where the desires are.

156
00:16:23,040 --> 00:16:28,583
[SPEAKER_00]: Yet, as I said, any engagement with sense-object is on account of you not wanting sense-desire to be there.

157
00:16:29,063 --> 00:16:35,047
[SPEAKER_00]: So, once you realize that, always, whenever there is a sense-desire, there is always a criteria of not wanting it.

158
00:16:35,447 --> 00:16:36,508
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's a good criteria.

159
00:16:37,148 --> 00:16:43,151
[SPEAKER_00]: It's just, then, choosing how you go about it, that's what determines it, whether it's going to be sensuality or not.

160
00:16:44,812 --> 00:16:49,195
[SPEAKER_00]: So, any sense-desire is not wanted, because it's unpleasant, because it's pressure.

161
00:16:50,535 --> 00:16:59,844
[SPEAKER_00]: If you give in towards it, trying to alleviate that pain through distracting yourself with sensual pursuit, you're making it worse.

162
00:16:59,904 --> 00:17:07,852
[SPEAKER_00]: If you don't go towards it, you might increase the pain initially, because, as I said, the leopard does get a degree of relief from scratching and burning it with fire.

163
00:17:09,373 --> 00:17:14,218
[SPEAKER_00]: So, once he stops engaging with that degree of relief, the things are going to go worse, but they're going to start healing.

164
00:17:14,538 --> 00:17:15,399
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's the point here.

165
00:17:17,130 --> 00:17:26,640
[SPEAKER_00]: Healing means you start to understand that doesn't matter how tiny prospect of pleasure arises in your mind, if it's directed at the senses, you know it's because it's painful.

166
00:17:27,519 --> 00:17:28,699
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why it's pleasure.

167
00:17:28,880 --> 00:17:29,840
[SPEAKER_00]: Because it's painful.

168
00:17:29,940 --> 00:17:30,900
[SPEAKER_00]: Because it pressures you.

169
00:17:31,441 --> 00:17:33,641
[SPEAKER_00]: Thus, the escape of it is pleasant.

170
00:17:34,162 --> 00:17:35,222
[SPEAKER_00]: Only because of the pressure.

171
00:17:35,682 --> 00:17:40,424
[SPEAKER_00]: Would you be elated at escaping the torture chamber, if you're not in prison there?

172
00:17:40,484 --> 00:17:41,804
[SPEAKER_00]: If you can come and go as you leave?

173
00:17:42,145 --> 00:17:42,585
[SPEAKER_00]: You wouldn't.

174
00:17:42,985 --> 00:17:48,187
[SPEAKER_00]: There would be no delight at the prospect of escaping it, because you are not in prison by it.

175
00:17:49,369 --> 00:17:50,350
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's exactly what I mean.

176
00:17:50,810 --> 00:18:05,845
[SPEAKER_00]: The genuine pleasure that sensuality offers, even actually while you're amidst of physical engagement with sensuality, the happiness of it, the joy, the pleasure, is the pleasure of pain felt on the level of the same things being present.

177
00:18:07,145 --> 00:18:12,968
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the happiness of sensuality is because of the painful presence of the same sensuality.

178
00:18:13,468 --> 00:18:19,510
[SPEAKER_00]: It's the same thing, just two different directions, and you keep choosing to look at the direction that distracts you from it.

179
00:18:20,251 --> 00:18:26,013
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why we keep insisting on the developing mindfulness of the peripheral and the context, because this is all found in the context.

180
00:18:26,053 --> 00:18:29,195
[SPEAKER_00]: As I said, you start with a thought of sensuality.

181
00:18:29,235 --> 00:18:30,335
[SPEAKER_00]: What is the context of it?

182
00:18:30,435 --> 00:18:34,517
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, it's the peripherally enduring context of desire for that.

183
00:18:35,846 --> 00:18:40,327
[SPEAKER_00]: Now, you get to discern the peripheral enduring context of the desire for this particular sense.

184
00:18:40,747 --> 00:18:42,727
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, you also get to discern further peripheral.

185
00:18:43,067 --> 00:18:51,409
[SPEAKER_00]: What are the characteristics of the presently enduring pressuring desire to do, to eat something, or touch something, or smell something of pleasant nature?

186
00:18:52,229 --> 00:18:55,750
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, the fact is that it's pressuring you unpleasantly.

187
00:18:56,910 --> 00:18:57,190
[SPEAKER_00]: Always.

188
00:18:57,830 --> 00:19:01,511
[SPEAKER_00]: Can you be pressured pleasantly and then go and seek the escape from it?

189
00:19:01,611 --> 00:19:01,731
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

190
00:19:01,791 --> 00:19:02,511
[SPEAKER_00]: Why would you do that?

191
00:19:02,531 --> 00:19:03,591
[SPEAKER_00]: There's nothing to escape from.

192
00:19:04,820 --> 00:19:06,981
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you are pressured by the pain of it.

193
00:19:07,081 --> 00:19:12,322
[SPEAKER_00]: That is the, like, as I said, inherent context of any sensual direction.

194
00:19:13,382 --> 00:19:15,903
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you will be going down to sensuality because you want to avoid pain.

195
00:19:16,543 --> 00:19:18,283
[SPEAKER_00]: So, then you realize, okay, I want to avoid pain.

196
00:19:18,343 --> 00:19:19,103
[SPEAKER_00]: That's absolutely fine.

197
00:19:19,804 --> 00:19:21,464
[SPEAKER_00]: What is the best way of going about it?

198
00:19:22,404 --> 00:19:25,085
[SPEAKER_00]: Going down to sensuality clearly keeps making it worse.

199
00:19:25,897 --> 00:19:34,268
[SPEAKER_00]: But if I were to restrain, so... so the mind can have a chance to develop in regard to this pressure, and is not pressured by it anymore.

200
00:19:34,989 --> 00:19:41,477
[SPEAKER_00]: When the mind is not pressured by the prospect of sensuality, the mind is not... well, the mind has surmounted the domain of sensuality.

201
00:19:43,110 --> 00:19:44,932
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's... we come back to how we started.

202
00:19:45,392 --> 00:19:46,233
[SPEAKER_00]: How to contemplate?

203
00:19:46,253 --> 00:19:50,837
[SPEAKER_00]: How to develop mind that would surmount the domain of sensualities?

204
00:19:51,017 --> 00:19:53,960
[SPEAKER_00]: Develop the mind that cannot be pressured by the possibility of sensuality.

205
00:19:54,000 --> 00:20:02,608
[SPEAKER_00]: In other words, develop the mind that will always be able to quickly, instantaneously, simultaneously discern sensual or isn't thought as pain.

206
00:20:03,749 --> 00:20:04,769
[SPEAKER_00]: And then he won't go near it.

207
00:20:05,670 --> 00:20:08,090
[SPEAKER_00]: You won't even have to then make the effort to not go near it.

208
00:20:08,190 --> 00:20:09,430
[SPEAKER_00]: He won't go near it.

209
00:20:10,031 --> 00:20:16,532
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's again what the Buddha would describe by the the leper who has now healed his leprosy and his wounds.

210
00:20:17,012 --> 00:20:25,154
[SPEAKER_00]: He said, well, imagine now that he's healed, skin normally covered, and now two strong men come and pick him up and try to carry him back into those burning embers.

211
00:20:26,074 --> 00:20:31,776
[SPEAKER_00]: Wouldn't he resist and kick and scream and and and like think anything else except going into the embers?

212
00:20:31,816 --> 00:20:32,656
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes, of course he would.

213
00:20:33,436 --> 00:20:34,557
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's exactly the point.

214
00:20:34,657 --> 00:20:44,042
[SPEAKER_00]: Even the slightest thought, the factual presence of possibility of sensuality, right in your face, access to sensual objects, will be pain.

215
00:20:44,282 --> 00:20:48,565
[SPEAKER_00]: Access to pain and the mind will shine, like, completely, completely shiver away from it.

216
00:20:48,585 --> 00:20:51,627
[SPEAKER_00]: It's just, it will not even entertain it for a second.

217
00:20:51,987 --> 00:20:57,070
[SPEAKER_00]: Because if it does entertain it for a second, means to that second, it still thinks it's pleasant first.

218
00:20:57,855 --> 00:20:59,216
[SPEAKER_00]: Not unpleasant first.

219
00:20:59,896 --> 00:21:13,962
[SPEAKER_00]: If it's unpleasant first, and the pleasure of it can never step outside of the context of the pain, means that pleasure is not pleasure, it's just distraction from pain, which is felt pleasantly only because of the perspective of the pain, greater pain.

220
00:21:15,083 --> 00:21:15,583
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's it.

221
00:21:15,703 --> 00:21:20,445
[SPEAKER_00]: You maintain the context, you maintain the mind above sensuality, and mind above ill will, for that matter.

222
00:21:20,905 --> 00:21:22,666
[SPEAKER_00]: Because ill will is equally directional.

223
00:21:23,206 --> 00:21:24,707
[SPEAKER_00]: Somebody, something upsets you,

224
00:21:26,081 --> 00:21:27,382
[SPEAKER_00]: That's already unpleasant.

225
00:21:27,962 --> 00:21:28,382
[SPEAKER_00]: What you do?

226
00:21:28,422 --> 00:21:29,623
[SPEAKER_00]: You want to get rid of the unpleasure.

227
00:21:29,643 --> 00:21:31,243
[SPEAKER_00]: How do you get rid of that displeasure?

228
00:21:31,884 --> 00:21:33,364
[SPEAKER_00]: By attacking that which upset you.

229
00:21:33,924 --> 00:21:38,566
[SPEAKER_00]: But if you realize that you cannot possibly be upset by that which comes through the sense objects.

230
00:21:38,947 --> 00:21:43,068
[SPEAKER_00]: You can only be upset by resisting the pressure of that which comes through sense objects.

231
00:21:43,088 --> 00:21:53,353
[SPEAKER_00]: Now you stop resisting, it means, yeah, as the Buddha said, you can literally endure sticks and stones, abuse, verbal abuse, physical abuse, and the mind will be incapable of anger.

232
00:21:53,813 --> 00:21:55,755
[SPEAKER_00]: Not like you have to keep it free from anger.

233
00:21:55,915 --> 00:21:59,119
[SPEAKER_00]: It will be incapable from anger because anger is painful.

234
00:21:59,679 --> 00:22:02,823
[SPEAKER_00]: Sticks and stones are not painful.

235
00:22:03,504 --> 00:22:06,587
[SPEAKER_00]: Objects of your senses are just agreeable or disagreeable.

236
00:22:06,987 --> 00:22:08,990
[SPEAKER_00]: They're not on the level of pain and suffering.

237
00:22:09,871 --> 00:22:13,194
[SPEAKER_00]: However, you being pressured by the

238
00:22:14,828 --> 00:22:17,592
[SPEAKER_00]: Possibility of escaping anger, that's where your suffering is.

239
00:22:18,093 --> 00:22:24,021
[SPEAKER_00]: You being pressured by the possibility of escaping the pain of of sensual desire, that's where the suffering is.

240
00:22:25,076 --> 00:22:32,844
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why these things are always underlined by the inherent craving for getting rid of it.

241
00:22:33,024 --> 00:22:38,029
[SPEAKER_00]: Getting rid of the central desire because it's unpleasant, getting rid of the pain because it's, again, it's unpleasant.

242
00:22:38,049 --> 00:22:41,933
[SPEAKER_00]: Sorry, getting rid of the aversion, pain of aversion because it's unpleasant.

243
00:22:42,013 --> 00:22:48,900
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you give in to anger, you give to central object, you give in to the offered direction of less pain, failing to see that less pain is still pain.

244
00:22:49,919 --> 00:22:52,901
[SPEAKER_00]: More pain, less pain, shares the nature of pain.

245
00:22:53,422 --> 00:23:01,248
[SPEAKER_00]: But, yes, if you forget that and start looking, well, as long as I avoid greater pain for the sake of less pain, then less pain will be regarded as pleasure.

246
00:23:02,089 --> 00:23:06,052
[SPEAKER_00]: And the extent of that pleasure is basically the extent of the pain that underlines it.

